</span><span class="enc-snip">Huáscar was Sapa Inca of the Inca Empire from about 1527 to 1532, ruling from Cuzco after the death of his father Huayna Capac.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/huastec-adolescent"><span class="enc-name">Huastec Adolescent</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Huastec Adolescent is a limestone statue of a young man, made by the Huastec people of north-eastern Mexico in the Postclassic period and found at Tamuín, San Luis…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/huastec-people"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/huastec-people.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Huastec people</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Huastec, or Teenek, are an Indigenous people of north-eastern Mexico who speak a Mayan language that separated from the other Mayan languages long ago.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/huasteca"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/huasteca.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Huasteca</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Huasteca is a region on the Gulf coast of Mexico, covering parts of Veracruz, San Luis Potosí, Tamaulipas, Hidalgo, Querétaro and Puebla.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/huave"><span class="enc-name">Huave</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Huave, who call themselves Ikoots, are an Indigenous people of the Pacific coast of the Isthmus of Tehuantepec in Oaxaca, Mexico, living around coastal lagoons…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/huayna-capac"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/huayna-capac.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Huayna Capac</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Huayna Capac was the Sapa Inca who ruled the Inca Empire at its greatest extent, from about 1493.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hubal"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hubal.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hubal</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Hubal was a god worshipped in pre-Islamic Mecca, whose statue of red stone with a golden hand stood inside the Kaaba.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hubei"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hubei.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hubei</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Hubei is a province of central China on the middle Yangtze, with its capital at Wuhan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hubert-ingraham"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hubert-ingraham.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hubert Ingraham</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hubert Ingraham is a Bahamian politician who served as prime minister of the Bahamas from 1992 to 2002 and again from 2007 to 2012.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hubert-lyautey"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hubert-lyautey.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hubert Lyautey</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hubert Lyautey was a French general and the first resident-general of the French protectorate in Morocco, from 1912 to 1925.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hubert-maga"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hubert-maga.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hubert Maga</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hubert Maga was a schoolteacher from northern Dahomey who became one of the colony's three leading politicians.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hubert-murray"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hubert-murray.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hubert Murray</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hubert Murray was an Australian lawyer who governed Papua as lieutenant-governor from 1908 until his death in 1940.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/huchuquan"><span class="enc-name">Huchuquan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Huchuquan was the last effectively independent chanyu of the Southern Xiongnu, ruling from 195 to 216.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hudhayl-ibn-khalaf-ibn-razin"><span class="enc-name">Hudhayl ibn Khalaf ibn Razin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hudhayl ibn Khalaf ibn Razin was a Berber chief of the Banu Razin who made Albarracín independent around 1012, as the Caliphate of Córdoba broke apart.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hudson-bay"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hudson-bay.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hudson Bay</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Hudson Bay is a large inland sea in north-eastern Canada.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hudson-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hudson-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hudson River</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Hudson River flows about 500 kilometres south from the Adirondack Mountains of New York to the Atlantic at New York City.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hudson-valley"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hudson-valley.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hudson Valley</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Hudson Valley is the valley of the Hudson River in New York, running north from New York City towards Albany.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hudsons-bay-company.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hudson's Bay Company</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Hudson's Bay Company was chartered by Charles II in 1670 with a monopoly of trade in all lands draining into Hudson Bay, a territory called Rupert's Land.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hudud-al-alam"><span class="enc-name">Hudud al-Alam</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Hudud al-Alam, 'The Limits of the World', is an anonymous Persian geography completed in 982 and dedicated to the Farighunid amir Abu'l-Harith Muhammad of Guzgan.</span></a></li></ul>
